ware  developers on your team ideally you'd be  a solid programmer yourself just look at  the founding teams behind every  successful social network there's always  at least one computer scientist on board  from the earliest stage now it is  possible to build a technology company  with only a business background but the  deck is going to be stacked against you  in a few key ways first off investors  are well aware that technical teams tend  to win so they will be less likely to  fund you and second developers don't  like working for non-technical people  it's really hard to get motivated to  work for someone who has no clue what  you actually do great technical minds  don't want to work with someone who will  just tell them to make the website fast  they want to partner with someone who  really understands the challenges of  building high performance software now  the good news is that it's pretty easy  to learn to code these days so even if  you have a business background you  should be able to study up and learn the  basics in a few months and if you're  building a piece of software i promise  it will be a great investment but what  if your company isn't primarily focused  on software development well then you  probably don't need to learn to code but  you still need to make sure your  founding team has the skills required to  deliver a high quality product clearly  software development wasn't an issue for  theranos what they needed was some sort  of major breakthrough in biology and  microfluidics most people agree that  we're still decades away from this type  of technology but when it arrives you  can be sure that the team that figures  it out will have deep technical  expertise in how biological systems work  so you've made sure that the world  actually needs your product that it's  possible to build your product and that  you have the team with the skills to  deliver that product now what well you  need to answer the level 2 question do  people want to buy your product  tons of talented entrepreneurs will  spend months building the next great  product only to launch it and get zero  sign ups on their landing page avoid  this situation at all costs it's  demoralizing and a huge waste of time  the faster you can get your product  built the faster you can get in the  hands of real users and start getting  feedback that's a big part of why it's  so important to have the technical  skills to build your product directly  within the founding team outsourcing  product development will slow you down  and add an extra layer of complexity to  your product iteration cycles you want  to be able to add a new feature to your  product and then go get feedback from  your users immediately and as soon as  your users give you their opinions you  need to incorporate that into the next  release immediately so how can you  conclusively prove that people want to  buy your product well you can ask them  to pay for it lots of entrepreneurs  start by asking friends family and  professional connections to test new  products it can be hard to get honest  feedback from people you know though so  this is where broader test marketing can  be helpful sharing a demo of what you're  building to popular sites like hacker  news reddit or product hunt can be a  great way to start testing your product  with people you don't know it's  important to understand the difference  between a user and a customer since they  aren't always the same person for  example facebook is a free service to  users the customers are advertisers who  want to reach those specific users if  you're building a business where the  user and the customer are different it  will be very important to validate that  everyone likes what you're building  after all there are tons of websites out  there with lots of users who can't  attract advertisers because they feature  objectionable content i've spent months  building projects i thought would be  successful only to launch them and  completely flop some companies spend  years making this mistake though the  short form video app quibby was in  development for years before going live  on the app store even though they had  spent over a billion dollars developing  the platform and content no one wanted  to pay for it the quibby team offered a  free trial but pretty much everyone who  tried the app wound up cancelling as  soon as the trial ended it was a  disappointing outcome for everyone  involved and it highlighted the  importance of validating demand for your  product so build your product quickly  adding only the few features you need to  prove the concept and then get it in the  hands of real customers as soon as you  have clear evidence that customers are  willing to pay for your product you'll  have a much easier time raising money  hiring employees and scaling your  business but getting customers to pay  for your product is only half the battle  plenty of entrepreneurs build a product  and establish a customer base only to  run head first into the level three  milestone profitability the entire point  of any business is to generate a profit  but a shocking amount of companies never  figure out how to do it now to be fair  i'm really talking about gross profit  here which is just revenue minus costs  it's completely normal for high growth  companies not to make net profits for  years but that's a completely different  metric let me explain gross profit  describes how much money a business  makes from just delivering their product  to customers it's just how much money  the customer pays minus how much it  costs to produce that particular product  for software companies gross profits are  typically very high since servers don't  cost very much to run but the gross  profits of grocery stores are usually  extremely low since the food they sell  to customers is expensive so you need to  really investigate how much your product  costs since this will be a critical  factor in determining if you can make a  positive gross profit selling your  product this might seem obvious but  you'd be surprised at how many startups  get this wrong they assume that they can  undercut the competition and then  magically reduce their costs in the long  term sometimes there really is a path to  reduce costs through automation or scale  economies your startup might be paying a  lot right now but you might be able to  swap in robots down the line or build a  larger manufacturing plant that can  produce products very cheaply it does  happen but often the business model is  just fundamentally flawed and there's no  path towards profitability just look at  what happened to moviepass the idea was  simple get people to pay for a  subscription and then let them go see  unlimited movies in theaters whenever  they want people loved moviepass the  product worked perfectly and customers  were definitely willing to pay the  monthly subscription fee so moviepass  had cleared the first two hurdles but  getting to gross profitability was  another issue entirely see every time a  moviepass customer swiped their  membership card at a theater moviepass  would have to pay for the ticket and  moviepass customers loved watching  movies the hope was that theaters would  be willing to charge lower prices or  maybe moviepass customers wouldn't see  so many movies but that never happened  instead the cost of each moviepass  customer exceeded the revenue they were  bringing in so the company was never  able to generate a gross profit it was a  tough situation for moviepass and the  company eventually had to file for  bankruptcy since they just couldn't find  a profitable business model even though  they had built a product that customers  loved none of that mattered since they  were losing so much money so always be  honest with yourself and your investors  is there really a path to making  reliable gross profits any company can  attract customers by selling something  at an unsustainably low price but if  th